# FlintTrade — desktop

> Tauri 2 native shell — backend sidecar + built terminal in one cross-OS installer
> Serves: single loopback origin | Branch: main (pre-release, no PRs)

## Quick Commands

```bash
make desktop-dev                                   # run the app in dev (builds the sidecar first)
make desktop-build                                 # full installers: frontend + sidecar + Tauri bundle
cd packages/apps/desktop/src-tauri && cargo check  # fast Rust validation
cd packages/apps/desktop/src-tauri && cargo test --lib   # Rust unit tests
```

## Architecture
- Tauri 2 shell in `src-tauri/src/lib.rs`; entry point `main.rs`; config `tauri.conf.json`
- Spawns the PyInstaller-frozen FlintTrade backend as a **sidecar** with a
  ready-port stdout handshake, then loads the built terminal from a single
  loopback origin (nothing listens externally)
- **Close-to-tray background runtime**: window close hides to the system tray;
  the backend, autonomous agent, and position monitoring keep running. Quit
  fully via the tray menu (QuitRequested AtomicBool). macOS dock-reopen
  restores the window
- Tray menu (Show/Quit + left-click toggle), global hotkey `Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+F`
- Native notifications: consumes `FLINTTRADE_NOTIFY\t<title>\t<body>` lines
  from the sidecar stdout (producer: `flinttrade_core/desktop_notify.py`,
  active only when `FLINTTRADE_DESKTOP=1`)
- Plugins: tray-icon, tauri-plugin-global-shortcut, tauri-plugin-notification

## House rules that bite
- The sidecar is the REAL backend — never bypass it with a second server
- Keep the loopback-only origin; adding an external listener is a safety bug
- The Kotak Neo SDK stays EXCLUDED from installers (redistribution vs use)
- Cross-platform verification belongs in the weekly nightly workflow, not
  per-push CI

